# Interesting! After sbumpc() returns -1 (EOF), the eof bit is NOT set on the stringstream!
# This is the root cause of the infinite loop.
# 
# The std::streambuf::sbumpc() returns EOF but doesn't set the eofbit on the stream.
# The stream's eofbit is only set by methods like read(), get(), getline(), etc.
# sbumpc() is a lower-level function that doesn't set eofbit.
#
# So after the '\n' is consumed, sbumpc() returns EOF but ss.eof() stays false!
# That's why the predict loop keeps running forever - !in.eof() is always true.
#
# The fix: we need to use stream's get() or read() methods which DO set eofbit,
# OR we need to check the sbumpc() return value ourselves and set eofbit.
#
# OR: We need to use a different approach for the predict loop.
# Instead of checking in.eof(), we check if getLine got any data.
#
# But we DO check "if (predictions.empty() && in.eof()) break"
# Since in.eof() never becomes true, this never breaks!
#
# The fix: use a flag returned by getLine to indicate real EOF

# Let me fix readWord to use in.get() which sets eofbit properly:
